Put a deadline on the event loop of a :mod:`gevent.subprocess` child that has
not ``exec``'d, so that it cannot stay there.

The child gets a hub of its own so that it cannot resume the parent's greenlets.
A hub that merely works, though, gives it somewhere to stay. A fork handler that
leaves anything at all on the loop and then waits for something only the parent
could provide waits forever: the loop has work to do, so it never raises
:exc:`~gevent.exceptions.LoopExit`, ``fork()`` never returns to
``_execute_child``, and no ``exec`` ever happens. The child lives on as a second
interpreter holding a clone of every descriptor the parent had at the fork.

One was found two hours after its fork, still wearing the parent's command line,
idle in ``epoll_wait`` on a five-second timer, 27 seconds of CPU spent, holding
all five of the application's pooled database connections and 52 TLS sockets.
Connections the parent believed closed were still open, because the child had
them too.

The parent is no better off, and this is what leaves nothing to notice. It blocks
reading the child's error pipe, waiting for the EOF that only ``exec`` or
``_exit`` produces. That read is inside :meth:`Popen.__init__`, so a ``timeout=``
on the spawn has not started counting and cannot end it.

Refusing the loop outright would be the obvious answer and is the wrong one. A
fork handler that yields is the ordinary case, not the pathological one:
``Thread.start()`` waits on an ``Event``, a patched lock may be held by another
greenlet, ``logging`` takes one, and the instrumentation that registers these
handlers (OpenTelemetry, Sentry, ``filelock``, ``coverage``) does all three. A
child that died on its first yield would take every spawn in such a process with
it.

So the loop runs, and a timer decides for how long. One second, against a window
that is normally shorter than a millisecond and a yield that costs microseconds.
The timer is unreferenced, so it does not hold the loop open: a handler with
nothing to wait for still gets ``LoopExit`` at once, as before, and the timer
fires only when something else is keeping the loop alive. That is precisely the
case that used to run for hours. The child then reports itself down the error
pipe and exits, and the spawn raises
:exc:`~subprocess.SubprocessError` in the parent instead of hanging.

A failed spawn is the cost. It is worth paying: the alternative was measured
costing the whole process, and a spawn that raises can be retried by an
application that can no longer be starved by one that never returns.
